er \\(H\\) is the intersection of \\(AD\\) and \\(BE\\), found to be \\((\\sqrt{3}, 2\\sqrt{3} - 3)\\).\n\nThe centroid \\(G\\) of \\(\\triangle ABH\\) is calculated by averaging the coordinates of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(H\\), resulting in \\(G\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}, \\frac{2\\sqrt{3} - 2}{3}\\right)\\).\n\nThe line \\(CG\\) is determined with slope \\(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) and equation \\(y = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x + \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). This line intersects \\(AB\\) at \\(M(1, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\) and \\(AD\\) at \\(N\\left(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{-3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\).\n\nTo verify \\(\\triangle AMN\\) is equilateral, we compute the distances \\(AM\\), \\(AN\\), and \\(MN\\):\n\n1. **Distance \\(AM\\)**:\n   \\[\n   AM = \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 + \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{16 - 8\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Distance \\(AN\\)**:\n   \\[\n   AN = \\left|1 - \\frac{-3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right| = \\frac{6 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n   Squaring this gives the same result as \\(AM\\).\n\n3. **Distance \\(MN\\)**:\n   \\[\n   MN = \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{16 - 8\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n   \\]\n\nAll distances are equal, confirming \\(\\triangle AMN\\) is equilateral.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\triangle AMN \\text{ is an equilateral triangle}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. In the acute triangle $\\triangle ABC$ with $\\angle B = 30^\\circ$, let $AD$ and $BE$ be the altitudes, and let $H$ be the orthocenter. Note that $BE$ is perpendicular to $AC$ and $AD$ is perpendicular to $BC$.\n2. Geometrically, connect point $A$ with point $E$ and point $B$ and point $D$.\n3. Perform a construction by drawing the altitude $CF$ from $C$ to $AB$. This makes $C$ the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle ABH\\) since any altitude drawn from a vertex in a triangle passes through the orthocenter.\n4. Define point $O$ as the circumcenter of $\\triangle ABH$. Note that the centroid $G$ of $\\triangle ABH$ lies on the Euler line which passes through $O$, $G$, and $C$ (Euler line property).\n5. Since $\\angle B = 30^\\circ$, we have $\\angle BAH = 60^\\circ$ (since $\\angle BAC + \\angle BAH = 90^\\circ$).\n6. The central angle subtended by $BAH$ at the circumcenter $O$ is twice the inscribed angle. Therefore, $\\angle BOH = 2\\angle BAH = 2 \\times 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$.\n7. Notice that $\\angle BCH = 90^\\circ - \\angle B = 60^\\circ$.\n8. The angles $\\angle BOH$ and $\\angle BCH$ add up to $180^\\circ$, which implies that $O$, $B$, $C$, and $H$ are concyclic (they lie on the same circle).\n9. We establish that $\\angle OCB = \\angle OHB = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(180^\\circ - \\angle BOH\\right)$ due to the cyclic properties of the quadrilateral $OBCGH$.\n10. Thus, $\\angle OCB = \\angle OHB = \\frac{1}{2}(180^\\circ - 120^\\circ) = 30^\\circ$.\n11. When looking at $\\triangle AMN$, note that $\\angle ANM = \\angle CND = 90^\\circ - \\angle OCB = 60^\\circ$.\n12. Finally, because $\\angle ANM = 60^\\circ$ and the aforementioned angle properties, $\\triangle AMN$ is an equilateral triangle (each angle in an equilateral triangle is $60^\\circ$).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\triangle AMN \\text{ is an equilateral triangle}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a square table in which some cells contain crosses. We will call a row odd if it contains an odd number of crosses. Similarly, a column is odd if it contains an odd number of crosses.\na) Is it possible for the table to have exactly 20 odd rows and 15 odd columns?\nb) Is it possible to arrange 126 crosses in a $16 \\times 16$ table so that all rows and columns are odd?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle these two problems step by step. Both are about square tables with crosses and the parity (odd or even) of the rows and columns. \n\nStarting with part a: \"Is it possible for the table to have exact