oots is -b and the product is c. This comes from Vieta's formulas. So maybe I can use that here.\n\nBut wait, the problem says each trinomial has two roots. That probably means each quadratic must have two distinct real roots. So, the discriminant of each quadratic must be positive. Let me note that down.\n\nFor the first quadratic, x\u00b2 -6x +4a, the discriminant D1 is (-6)\u00b2 - 4*1*4a = 36 - 16a. For two distinct real roots, D1 must be greater than 0. So, 36 -16a > 0 \u2192 16a < 36 \u2192 a < 36/16 \u2192 a < 9/4. That's 2.25.\n\nFor the second quadratic, x\u00b2 +ax +6, the discriminant D2 is a\u00b2 - 4*1*6 = a\u00b2 -24. For two distinct real roots, D2 > 0, so a\u00b2 -24 > 0 \u2192 a\u00b2 > 24 \u2192 a > sqrt(24) or a < -sqrt(24). sqrt(24) simplifies to 2*sqrt(6), which is approximately 4.899. So, a must be greater than about 4.899 or less than -4.899. But we already have a condition from the first quadratic that a must be less than 9/4 (2.25). So combining these, since a < 9/4 and a must also be less than -sqrt(24) or greater than sqrt(24). Wait, but sqrt(24) is approximately 4.899, which is greater than 9/4 (2.25). So, the only possible values of 'a' that satisfy both discriminant conditions would be a < -sqrt(24) because a can't be greater than sqrt(24) as it's already restricted to be less than 2.25. So, the first condition gives a < 9/4, and the second discriminant requires a < -sqrt(24) or a > sqrt(24), but since a < 9/4, the overlapping region is a < -sqrt(24). So, a must be less than -2*sqrt(6) (since sqrt(24) is 2*sqrt(6)). So that's a key point. So, the possible 'a' values must be less than -2*sqrt(6). Let me check that again. If the first quadratic requires a < 9/4 (~2.25), and the second quadratic requires a < -sqrt(24) (~-4.899) or a > sqrt(24) (~4.899), but since a < 9/4, the overlap is only a < -sqrt(24). So that's the first part, but we need to confirm this when solving the rest of the problem.\n\nBut the main condition is the sum of the