0f724478=6*24478=146868, remainder 164833-146868=17965.\n\nGCD(24478,17965). 24478 -17965=6513.\n\nGCD(17965,6513). 17965 \u00f76513=2*6513=13026, remainder 17965-13026=4939.\n\nGCD(6513,4939). 6513-4939=1574.\n\nGCD(4939,1574). 4939 \u00f71574=3*1574=4722, remainder 4939-4722=217.\n\nGCD(1574,217). 1574 \u00f7217=7*217=1519, remainder 1574-1519=55.\n\nGCD(217,55). 217 \u00f755=3*55=165, remainder 217-165=52.\n\nGCD(55,52). 55-52=3.\n\nGCD(52,3). 52 \u00f73=17*3=51, remainder 1.\n\nGCD(3,1). 1.\n\nTherefore, GCD(354144,164833)=1. So x\u2085 is -354144/164833 in reduced form. Therefore, the fractions are getting more complicated each time, but importantly, since each x_{n} is a fraction p_n/q_n where p_n and q_n are coprime integers (with possible negative sign), and since the recursion involves p_{n+1}=2 p_n q_n and q_{n+1}=p_n\u00b2 - q_n\u00b2, which in the case of x\u2081=2/1, all subsequent terms will have denominators and numerators that are integers. Since at each step, p_{n+1} and q_{n+1} are defined as such, and since we have shown in the first few steps that x_n never becomes \u00b11 (as they are fractions not equal to 1 or -1), and since each step's x_{n} is constructed from previous integers, it's impossible for x_n to ever be 1 or -1, because that would require p_n = \u00b1q_n, but given the recursion, starting from x\u2081=2/1, which is p\u2081=2, q\u2081=1, and p_n and q_n are coprime at each step (as GCD(p_{n+1}, q_{n+1}) divides GCD(2 p_n q_n, p_n\u00b2 - q_n\u00b2). Since p_n and q_n are coprime, GCD(2 p_n q_n, p_n\u00b2 - q_n\u00b2). Let's suppose that a prime divides both 2 p_n q_n and p_n\u00b2 - q_n\u00b2. If the prime divides p_n, then it divides p_n\u00b2 - q_n\u00b2, so it divides q_n\u00b2, which contradicts p_n and q_n being coprime. Similarly, if the prime divides q_n, same reasoning. Therefore, GCD(p_{n+1}, q_{n+1}) divides 2. So unless 2 divides both p_{n+1} and q_{n+1}, they are coprime. But p_{n+1}=2 p_n q_n, which is even, and q_{n+1}