o the inequality:\n\n(S2)^2 /3 -2S2 +1 \u22650.\n\nMultiply by 3: (S2)^2 -6S2 +3 \u22650.\n\nThis quadratic in S2: Let\u2019s compute its discriminant: 36 -12 =24. So roots at [6 \u00b1\u221a24]/2 = [6 \u00b12\u221a6]/2=3\u00b1\u221a6\u22483\u00b12.45. So approximately 0.55 and5.45.\n\nBut since a, b, c are non-negative and sum to1, S2 =a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 \u2264 (a +b +c)^2 =1. Because for non-negative variables, sum of squares \u2264 square of sum. So S2 \u22641. Therefore, our quadratic (S2)^2 -6S2 +3 would be \u22650 when S2 \u22643 -\u221a6\u22480.55 or S2 \u22653 +\u221a6\u22485.45. But S2 \u22641, so only the first interval applies. However, 3 -\u221a6\u22480.55, so if S2 \u22640.55, then the quadratic is \u22650, but if S2 >0.55, then it is negative.\n\nBut we need to check whether for S2 in [0.55,1], whether the original inequality S4 -2S2 +1 \u22650 holds. However, since we only have S4 \u2265(S2)^2 /3, if we use that estimate, for S2 >0.55, the quadratic becomes negative, so our bound is not sufficient. Therefore, this approach might not work.\n\nAlternative approach: Maybe note that since a +b +c=1, and variables are non-negative, we can use Lagrange multipliers to find the minimal value.\n\nLet\u2019s set up the function to minimize: f(a,b,c) = (1 -a\u00b2)^2 + (1 -b\u00b2)^2 + (1 -c\u00b2)^2.\n\nSubject to the constraint g(a,b,c)=a +b +c -1=0.\n\nUsing Lagrange multipliers, the gradient of f should be proportional to the gradient of g.\n\nCompute partial derivatives:\n\ndf/da = 2(1 -a\u00b2)(-2a) = -4a(1 -a\u00b2),\n\nSimilarly, df/db = -4b(1 -b\u00b2),\n\ndf/dc = -4c(1 -c\u00b2).\n\nThe gradient of g is (1,1,1). So the Lagrange multiplier condition:\n\n-4a(1 -a\u00b2) = \u03bb,\n\n-4b(1 -b\u00b2) = \u03bb,\n\n-4c(1 -c\u00b2) = \u03bb.\n\nTherefore, -4a(1 -a\u00b2) = -4b(1 -b\u00b2) = -4c(1 -c\u00b2).\n\nThus, a(1 -a\u00b2)=b(1 -b\u00b2)=c(1 -c\u00b2).\n\nSo either all variables are equal, or some are equal and others are zero.\n\nCase1: All variables equal: a =b =c=1/3.\n\nAs checked earlier, the value is \u22482.37>2.\n\nCase2: Two variables equal, third different. Suppose a =b \u2260c.\n\nThen from the equations: a(1 -a\u00b2)=c(1 -c\u00b2).\n\nAlso, 2a +c =1.\n\nNeed to solve these equations. Let me set a =b, so c=1 -2a.\n\nSo substituting into a(1 -a\u00b2)=c(1 -c\u00b2):\n\na(1 -a\u00b2) = (1 -2a)(1 - (1 -2a)^2).\n\nCompute the right-hand side:\n\n(1 -2a)(1 - (1 -4a +4a\u00b2)) = (1 -2a)(4a -4a\u00b2) = (1 -2a)(4a(1 -a)).\n\nSo:\n\na(1 -a\u00b2) =4a(1 -a)(1 -2a).\n\nAssuming a \u22600 (if a=0, then c=1, b=0, which is a corner case already considered), divide both sides by a:\n\n1 -a\u00b2 =4(1 -a)(1 -2a).\n\nExpand the right side:\n\n4(1 -a -2a +2a\u00b2)=4(1 -3a +2a\u00b2)=4 -12a +8a\u00b2.\n\nThus, equation becomes:\n\n1 -a\u00b2 =4 -12a +8a\u00b2,\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n1 -a\u00b2 -4 +12a -8a\u00b2=0,\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n-9a\u00b2 +12a -3=0,\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n9a\u00b2 -12a +3=0,\n\nDivide by3:\n\n3a\u00b2 -4a +1=0,\n\nSolve quadratic equation:\n\na=(4\u00b1\u221a(16-12))/6=(4\u00b12)/6.\n\nThus, a=(4+2)/6=1 or a=(4-2)/6=1/3.\n\nIf a=1, then c=1-2*1= -1, which is invalid since c\u22650.\n\nIf a=1/3, then c=1-2*(1/3)=1/3. So a=b=c=1/3, which is the symmetric case again. So no new solutions here.\n\nCase3: One variable is 1, others zero.\n\nSuppose a=1, b=c=0. Check if this is a critical point. The partial derivatives would be:\n\ndf/da at a=1: -4*1*(1 -1)=0,\n\ndf/db at b=0: -4*0*(1 -0)=0,\n\nSimilarly df/dc=0. So the gradient is (0,0,0), which satisfies Lagrange condition with \u03bb=0. But the constraint is a +b +c=1. So this is a valid critical point. Indeed, when we computed earlier, the value at this point is exactly 2, which is the minimum.\n\nAnother case: Two variables zero, third is1. Which is same as above.\n\nAlternatively, check if there's a case with one variable zero, others non-zero.\n\nSuppose c=0, then a +b=1, and need to minimize (1 -a\u00b2)^2 + (1 -b\u