 let me check with another method to be sure. Alternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the calculations.\n\nWait, let's verify some of the individual terms.\n\nFor |A|: x\u2081>9. Substitute y\u2081=x\u2081 -9, equation y\u2081 +x\u2082 +x\u2083 +x\u2084=14. Positive solutions: C(14-1,4-1)=C(13,3)=286. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, |B|: x\u2082>8. Substitute y\u2082=x\u2082 -8. Total=15. C(14,3)=364. Correct.\n\n|C|: x\u2083>7. Total=16. C(15,3)=455. Correct.\n\n|D|:x\u2084>6. Total=17. C(16,3)=560. Correct.\n\nSo first four terms are correct.\n\nPairwise intersections:\n\nA\u2229B: total 6. C(5,3)=10. Correct.\n\nA\u2229C: total 7. C(6,3)=20. Correct.\n\nA\u2229D: total 8. C(7,3)=35. Correct.\n\nB\u2229C: total 8. C(7,3)=35. Correct.\n\nB\u2229D: total 9. C(8,3)=56. Correct.\n\nC\u2229D: total 10. C(9,3)=84. Correct.\n\nSum pairwise: 10+20+35+35+56+84=240. Correct.\n\nTriple intersections: All lead to totals that are negative or too low, so zero. Correct.\n\nFour-way intersection: Negative, zero. Correct.\n\nTherefore, inclusion-exclusion gives invalid solutions=1425.\n\nTotal solutions=1540 -1425=115. So the answer is 115.\n\nBut let me check with generating functions as an alternative approach to verify.\n\nThe generating function for each variable with constraints:\n\nFor x\u2081: maximum 9, positive integers. So generating function is x + x\u00b2 + ... +x\u2079.\n\nSimilarly, x\u2082: x + ... +x\u2078,\n\nx\u2083: x + ... +x\u2077,\n\nx\u2084: x + ... +x\u2076.\n\nThe generating function is (x +x\u00b2+\u2026+x\u2079)(x +\u2026+x\u2078)(x +\u2026+x\u2077)(x +\u2026+x\u2076).\n\nWe need the coefficient of x\u00b2\u00b3 in this product.\n\nAlternatively, we can write each as (x(1 -x^9)/(1 -x)) etc.\n\nSo the generating function becomes:\n\n[x(1 -x^9)/(1 -x)] * [x(1 -x^8)/(1 -x)] * [x(1 -x^7)/(1 -x)] * [x(1 -x^6)/(1 -x)].\n\nWhich is x\u2074(1 -x^9)(1 -x^8)(1 -x^7)(1 -x^6) / (1 -x)^4.\n\nWe need the coefficient of x^{23} in this, which is the same as the coefficient of x^{23 -4}=x^{19} in (1 -x^9)(1 -x^8)(1 -x^7)(1 -x^6)/(1 -x)^4.\n\nSo expand the numerator:\n\n(1 -x^6 -x^7 -x^8 -x^9 +x^{6+7} +x^{6+8} +x^{6+9} +x^{7+8} +x^{7+9} +x^{8+9} -x^{6+7+8} -x^{6+7+9} -x^{6+8+9} -x^{7+8+9} +x^{6+7+8+9}) / (1 -x)^4.\n\nBut this seems complicated. Alternatively, expand (1 -x^6)(1 -x^7)(1 -x^8)(1 -x^9):\n\nFirst multiply (1 -x^6)(1 -x^7) = 1 -x^6 -x^7 +x^{13}.\n\nThen multiply by (1 -x^8): 1 -x^6 -x^7 +x^{13} -x^8 +x^{14} +x^{15} -x^{21}.\n\nThen multiply by (1 -x^9): (1 -x^6 -x^7 +x^{13} -x^8 +x^{14} +x^{15} -x^{21})(1 -x^9).\n\nMultiplying each term:\n\n1*(1 -x^9) = 1 -x^9,\n\n(-x^6)*(1 -x^9) = -x^6 +x^{15},\n\n(-x^7)*(1 -x^9) = -x^7 +x^{16},\n\nx^{13}*(1 -x^9) =x^{13} -x^{22},\n\n(-x^8)*(1 -x^9)= -x^8 +x^{17},\n\nx^{14}*(1 -x^9)=x^{14} -x^{23},\n\nx^{15}*(1 -x^9)=x^{15} -x^{24},\n\n(-x^{21})*(1 -x^9)= -x^{21} +x^{30}.\n\nCombine all these terms:\n\n1 -x^9 -x^6 +x^{15} -x^7 +x^{16} +x^{13} -x^{22} -x^8 +x^{17} +x^{14} -x^{23} +x^{15} -x^{24} -x^{21} +x^{30}.\n\nNow collect like terms:\n\nConstant term: 1.\n\nNegative x^6: -x^6.\n\nNegative x^7: -x^7.\n\nNegative x^8: -x^8.\n\nNegative x^9: -x^9.\n\nPositive x^{13}: +x^{13}.\n\nPositive x^{14}: +x^{14}.\n\nTwo x^{15}: +2x^{15}.\n\nPositive x^{16}: +x^{16}.\n\nPositive x^{17}: +x^{17}.\n\nNegative x^{21}: -x^{21}.\n\nNegative x^{22}: -x^{22}.\n\nNegative x^{23}: -x^{23}.\n\nNegative x^{24}: -x^{24}.\n\nPositive x^{30}: +x^{30}.\n\nSo the expanded numerator is:\n\n1 -x^6 -x^7 -x^8 -x^9 +x^{13} +x^{14} +2x^{15} +x^{16} +x^{17} -x^{21} -x^{22} -x^{23} -x^{24} +x^{30}.\n\nNow, the generating function is this numerator divided by (1 -x)^4.\n\nWe need the coefficient of x^{19} in numerator/(1 -x)^4. Since the numerator is a polynomial, we can write the coefficient as the sum of coefficients of the numerator multiplied by the coefficients from 1/(1 -x)^4 shifted accordingly.\n\nRecall that 1/(1 -x)^4 = \u03a3 C(n +3,3) x^n.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{19} in numerator/(1 -x)^4 is:\n\nCoeff(x^0 in numerator) * C(19 +3,3) +\n\nCoeff(x^1) * C(19 -1 +3,3) + ... up to Coeff(x^{19})*C(3,3).\n\nBut since the numerator has terms up to x^{30}, but we are only interested up to x^{19}, the terms beyond x^{19} in the numerator won't contribute. Let's list the terms in the numerator up to x^{19}:\n\n1 (x^0),\n\n- x^6,\n\n- x^7,\n\n- x^8,\n\n- x^9,\n\n+ x^{13},\n\n+ x^{14},\n\n+ 2x^{15},\n\n+ x^{16},\n\n+ x^{17},\n\nand then -x^{21} etc., which are beyond x^{19}.\n\nSo, the coefficients for x^k in the numerator where k \u226419 are:\n\nk=0: 1\n\nk=6: -1\n\nk=7: -1\n\nk=8: -1\n\nk=9: -1\n\nk=13: +1\n\nk=14: +1\n\nk=15: +2\n\nk=16: +1\n\nk=17: +1\n\nFor other k from 0 to19, coefficient is 0.\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of x^{19} in numerator/(1 -x)^4 is:\n\n1*C(19 +3,3) +\n\n(-1)*C(19 -6 +3,3) +\n\n(-1)*C(19 -7 +3,3) +\n\n(-1)*C(19 -8 +3,3) +\n\n(-1)*C(19 -9 +3,3) +\n\n1*C(19 -13 +3,3) +\n\n1*C(19 -14 +3,3) +\n\n2*C(19 -15 +3,3) +\n\n1*C(19 -16 +3,3) +\n\n1*C(19 -17 +3,3)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: C(22,3)=1540.\n\nSecond term: -C(16,3). C(16,3)=560. So -560.\n\nThird term: -C(15,3)= -455.\n\nFourth term: -C(14,3)= -364.\n\nFifth term: -C(13,3)= -286.\n\nSixth term: +C(9,3)=84.\n\nSeventh term: +C(8,3)=56.\n\nEighth term: +2*C(7,3)=2*35=70.\n\nNinth term: +C(6,3)=20.\n\nTenth term: +C(5,3)=10.\n\nNow sum all these:\n\n1540 -560 -455 -364 -286 +84 +56 +70 +20 +10.\n\nLet's compute step by step:\n\nStart with 1540.\n\nSubtract 560: 1540 -560=980.\n\nSubtract 455: 980 -455=525.\n\nSubtract 364: 525 -364=161.\n\nSubtract 286: 161 -286= -125.\n\nAdd 84: -125 +84= -41.\n\nAdd 56: -41 +56=15.\n\nAdd 70:15 +70=85.\n\nAdd 20:85 +20=105.\n\nAdd 10:105 +10=115.\n\nSo the coefficient is 115. Therefore, the number of solutions is 115, which matches the inclusion-exclusion result.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 115.\n\nI think this confirms that the correct number is 115. Both methods agree. So despite the complexity of inclusion-exclusion, the generating function approach also leads to the same answer, which gives me confidence that it's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of positive integer solutions is \\boxed{115}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the number of positive integer solutions to the equation \\(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3} + x_{4} = 23\\) with the constraints \\(x_{1} \\leqslant 9\\), \\(x_{2} \\leqslant 8\\), \\(x_{3} \\leqslant 7\\), and \\(x_{4} \\leqslant 6\\), we use the principle of inclusion-exclusion.\n\n1. **Total solutions without constraints**: The number of positive integer solutions to \\(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4 = 23\\) is given by \\(\\binom{22}{3} = 1540\\).\n\n2. **Subtract invalid solutions**:\n   - **Individual violations**:\n     - \\(x_1 > 9\\): \\(\\binom{13}{3} = 286\\)\n     - \\(x_2 > 8\\): \\(\\binom{14}{3} = 364\\)\n     - \\(x_3 > 7\\): \\(\\binom{15}{3} = 455\\)\n     - \\(x_4 > 6\\): \\(\\binom{16}{3} = 560\\)\n     - Total: \\(286 + 364 + 455 + 560 = 1665\\)\n\n   - **Pairwise violations**:\n     - \\(x_1 > 9\\) and \\(x_2 > 8\\): \\(\\binom{5}{3} = 10\\)\n     - \\(x_1 > 9\\) and \\(x_3 > 7\\): \\(\\binom{6}{3} = 20\\)\n     - \\(x_1 > 9\\) and \\(x_4 > 6\\): \\(\\binom{7}{3} = 35\\)\n     - \\(x_2 > 8\\) and \\(x_3 > 7\\): \\(\\binom{7}{3} = 35\\)\n     - \\(x_2 > 8\\) and \\(x_4 > 6\\): \\(\\binom{8}{3} = 56\\)\n     - \\(x_3 > 7\\) and \\(x_4 > 6\\): \\(\\binom{9}{3} = 84\\)\n     - Total: \\(10 + 20 + 35 + 35 + 56 + 84 = 240\\)\n\n   - **Triple and four-way violations**: All triple and four-way intersections result in negative sums, hence contribute 0.\n\n3. **Valid solutions**:\n   - Subtract invalid solutions from total: \\(1540 - (1665 - 240) = 1540 - 1425 = 115\\).\n\n4. **Verification with generating functions**:\n   - The generating function approach confirms the coefficient of \\(x^{23}\\) in the product of the constrained polynomials is 115.\n\nThus, the number of positive integer solutions is \\(\\boxed{115}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n\nTo find the number of positive integer solutions for the equation \\( x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4 = 23 \\), given that \\( x_1 \\leqslant 9 \\), \\( x_2 \\leqslant 8 \\), \\( x_3 \\leqslant 7 \\), and \\( x_4 \\leqslant 6 \\), we use the concept of generating functions.\n\n1. Introduce a generating function for the number of positive integer solutions satisfying \\( x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4 = r \\) where \\( x_1 \\leqslant 9 \\), \\( x_2 \\leqslant 8 \\), \\( x_3 \\leqslant 7 \\), and \\( x_4 \\leqslant 6 \\).\n\n2. The generating functions for each variable \\( x_i \\) are given by:\n   \\[\n   \\left( x + x^2 + x^3 + \\cdots + x^9 \\right), \\left( x + x^2 + x^3 + \\cdots + x^8 \\right), \\left( x + x^2 + x^3 + \\cdots + x^7 \\right), \\left( x + x^2 + x^3 + \\cdots + x^6 \\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. The product of these generating functions is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( x + x^2 + \\cdots + x^9 \\right) \\left( x + x^2 + \\cdots + x^8 \\right) \\left( x + x^2 + \\cdots + x^7 \\right) \\left( x + x^2 + \\cdots + x^6 \\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. Rewriting this product using the sum of a geometric series, we get:\n   \\[\n   x^4 \\cdot \\frac{(1 - x^{9})(1 - x^{8})(1 - x^{7})(1 - x^{6})}{(1 - x)^4}\n   \\]\n\n5. The goal is to find the coefficient of \\( x^{19} \\) in the expansion of:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(1 - x^{9})(1 - x^{8})(1 - x^{7})(1 - x^{6})}{(1 - x)^4}\n   \\]\n\n6. We need to expand:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{(1 - x)^4} = \\sum_{r = 0}^{\\infty} \\binom{r + 3}{3} x^r\n   \\]\n\n7. Compute:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = (1 - x^6 - x^7 - x^8 - x^9 + x^{13} + x^{14} + 2x^{15} + x^{16} + x^{17}) \\sum_{r = 0}^{\\infty} \\binom{r + 3}{3} x^r\n   \\]\n\n8. The coefficient of \\( x^{19} \\) is obtained by collecting and summing the relevant terms. Specifically, we look at:\n   \\[\n   \\sum \\left( \\text{coefficients from } \\binom{r + 3}{3} x^r \\right) - \\left( \\text{coefficients from } \\text{higher powers caused by } x^6, x^7, \\ldots \\right)\n   \\]\n\n9. Hence, the required coefficient of \\( x^{19} \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{22}{3} - \\binom{16}{3} - \\binom{15}{3} - \\binom{14}{3} - \\binom{13}{3} + \\binom{9}{3} + \\binom{8}{3} + 2 \\binom{7}{3} + \\binom{6}{3} + \\binom{5}{3}\n   \\]\n\n10. Calculate these binomial coefficients:\n    \\[\n    \\binom{22}{3} = 1540, \\quad \\binom{16}{3} = 560, \\quad \\binom{15}{3} = 455, \\quad \\binom{14}{3} = 364, \\quad \\binom{13}{3} = 286\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\binom{9}{3} = 84, \\quad \\binom{8}{3} = 56, \\quad \\binom{7}{3} = 35, \\quad 2 \\binom{7}{3} = 70, \\quad \\binom{6}{3} = 20, \\quad \\binom{5}{3} = 10\n    \\]\n\n11. Summing these values:\n    \\[\n    1540 - 560 - 455 - 364 - 286 + 84 + 56 + 70 + 20 + 10 = 115\n    \\]\n\nConclusion: The number of positive integer solutions is \\( \\boxed{115} \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The ends of a segment of fixed length move along two skew perpendicular lines. What trajectory does the midpoint of this segment follow?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out the trajectory of the midpoint of a segment whose ends are moving along two skew perpendicular lines. Hmm, let's start by recalling what skew lines are. Skew lines are lines in three-dimensional space that are neither parallel nor intersecting. They lie in different planes. Since the lines are also perpendicular, they must be skew and perpendicular, meaning the direction vectors of the two lines are perpendicular to each other.\n\nThe problem states that the ends of a fixed-length segment move along these two lines. Let me visualize this. Suppose we have two skew lines, let's call them line L1 and line L2. They are perpendicular and don't intersect. The segment has a fixed length, say length 2a (I'll use 2a because the midpoint will then be at a distance a from each end, which might simplify things). The endpoints are moving along L1 and L2, and the midpoint M traces some trajectory. I need to find what that trajectory is.\n\nFirst, I should probably set up a coordinate system to model this problem. Let's choose coordinates such that the two skew lines can be represented in a convenient way. Since they are skew and perpendicular, we can align them with coordinate axes but offset appropriately.\n\nLet me recall that in 3D space, two skew lines can be represented parametrically. Let's consider line L1 along the x-axis and line L2 along the y-axis but shifted along the z-axis. Wait, but if they are skew and perpendicular, perhaps aligning them with the x-axis and y-axis but with a shift in another axis. Let me think.\n\nSuppose line L1 is the x-axis, which can be parametrized as (t, 0, 0) where t \u2208 \u211d. Then line L2 needs to be a line that is skew to L1 and perpendicular. To make them perpendicular, their direction vectors should be perpendicular. The direction vector of L1 is (1, 0, 0). So the direction vector of L2 should be (0, 1, 0) or (0, 0, 1). But if L2 is along the y-axis, shifted in z, then it would be parametrized as (0, s, c) where c is a constant shift. Wait, but then the lines L1 and L2 would not intersect, since L1 is (t, 0, 0) and L2 is (0, s, c). The distance between them is |c| / sqrt(0^2 + 0^2 + 1^2) ) = |c|, which is the minimum distance between the two lines. Since they are skew, c \u2260 0.\n\nBut the problem says the lines are skew and perpendicular. The direction vectors of L1 and L2 must be perpendicular. So if L1 is along the x-axis with direction vector (1, 0, 0), then L2 must have direction vector (0, a, b) such that the dot product with (1, 0, 0) is zero. So any direction vector for L2 with zero x-component would be perpendicular. The simplest choice is direction vector (0, 1, 0), but shifted in z, so line L2 could be parametrized as (0, s, c), as before.\n\nAlternatively, to make it even more symmetric, maybe shift L2 along the z-axis. So let's set up coordinates such that:\n\n- Line L1 is the x-axis: (t, 0, 0), t \u2208 \u211d\n\n- Line L2 is the line parallel to the y-axis but sh