mber of edges in this graph while ensuring it can be embedded in the grid.\n\n### Key Steps:\n1. **Graph Representation**: The problem reduces to finding the minimum number of edges in a connected planar graph with 23 nodes. A connected planar graph with \\( V \\) nodes must have at least \\( V - 1 \\) edges (a tree).\n2. **Star Graph Approach**: Arrange one color as the central node (e.g., color 1) and connect it to the remaining 22 colors. This forms a star graph where:\n   - The central color is adjacent to all other colors.\n   - Each non-central color is only adjacent to the central color.\n   - This results in **22 good pairs**, as each edge represents a unique pair between the central color and one other color.\n3. **Grid Embedding**: Place the central color in multiple cells, each adjacent to a different non-central color. Ensure non-central colors are spaced apart to avoid unintended adjacencies. For example:\n   - Use the central color as a background, with isolated cells of other colors surrounded by the central color.\n   - This configuration ensures no additional adjacencies between non-central colors.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe minimal connected graph (a tree) with 23 nodes requires **22 edges**, corresponding to **22 good pairs**. This is achievable in the grid using a star-like pattern, where one color serves as the hub for all others. Thus, the minimum number of good pairs is:\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{22}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Interpretation of the problem**: \n    Given a grid colored in 23 different colors, we need to determine the minimum number of \"good pairs\" of colors. A pair of colors is considered \"good\" if there exists at least one pair of adjacent cells (neighbors) painted in these two colors.\n\n2. **Modeling the problem as a graph**:\n    - Imagine each color as a vertex in a graph. Hence, we have 23 vertices corresponding to the 23 colors.\n    - Draw an edge between two vertices if their corresponding colors form a go